--TEST--
A `readonly` property is written by its class's constructor and by nothing else, declared or promoted (`rule:classes/readonly-is-written-once`)
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `rule:classes/lateinit-restrictions` states `readonly`'s contract while refusing to combine it with
// `lateinit`: "assigned exactly once, and that assignment happens during
// construction". The constructor's own write below is the assignment that
// contract allows; every other write is refused where it is written, so the
// promise holds without a run-time check.
class Widget {
    public readonly uint $id;

    public function constructor(uint $id) {
        $this->id = $id;
    }

    // Inside the declaring class, and still after construction: `bump` is
    // called on a built instance, so the property has already been written
    // once. This is the half PHP admits — it allows an initializing write
    // from any method of the declaring class and throws only on the second
    // one — and E0782 is where the two part company.
    public function bump(): void {
        $this->id = $this->id + 1;
    }
}

// A promoted constructor parameter carries the modifier the same way, so the
// refusal has to read it off the promotion and not only off a declaration.
class Label {
    public function constructor(public readonly string $text) {}
}

var $l = new Label("a");
$l->text = "b";
// covers: lang:classes/properties
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0782]: `Widget::$id` is `readonly`, so only `Widget`'s constructor writes it
%A
error[E0782]: `Label::$text` is `readonly`, so only `Label`'s constructor writes it
%A
error: aborting due to 2 errors
To read what a code means and how to fix it, run `nvs agent show <code>`.
